## Review Summary

I checked every numeric chain in the brief (unit pricing, brand total, platform revenue, contribution, monthly-scale extrapolation, budget sum) and all arithmetic is internally consistent — no computational errors found. Issues below are about disclosure clarity and omitted implications, not math mistakes.

```json
{
  "verdict": "acceptable_with_minor_disclosures",
  "issues": [
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "claim": "Monthly scale: 100 brands × 2 campaigns × 10,000 confirmed rewards × 15% = 300,000 MYR platform revenue",
      "reason": "This is the largest number in the deck but doesn't carry forward the unit-economics variable cost/contribution assumptions established earlier (600 cost per 10,000-reward campaign). A reader could mistake 300,000 MYR for near-profit rather than gross platform revenue, and the underlying 100-brand/2-campaign assumption has no demand validation since the platform hasn't launched.",
      "fix": "Restate the 100/2/10,000 assumptions immediately next to the figure, label it 'illustrative, not a forecast,' and either show the scaled variable cost/contribution (~200×600=120,000 cost, ~180,000 contribution) or explicitly note contribution is not carried through at this scale."
    },
    {
      "severity": "low",
      "claim": "Contribution 900 (excl. fixed cost/tax); at 1,500 variable cost, contribution is zero",
      "reason": "Two different variable-cost assumptions (600 and 1,500) are presented in one line without separation, which can read as an inconsistency to a fast reader rather than a deliberate sensitivity check.",
      "fix": "Split into a labeled base case ('900 at 600 variable cost') and a separate labeled breakeven sensitivity ('contribution reaches zero if variable cost rises to 1,500')."
    },
    {
      "severity": "low",
      "claim": "One creator with 50,000 views earns 250 MYR, subject to limits",
      "reason": "The 'limits' are undefined, while the real TJR benchmark discloses explicit caps (min 5,000 views, max $750/clip). This inconsistency in rigor leaves budget-exhaustion risk (a single creator draining the 10,000 MYR pool) unaddressed.",
      "fix": "State an explicit min/max view or payout cap per creator/clip, mirroring the disclosed structure of the real-world comparable."
    },
    {
      "severity": "low",
      "claim": "Monthly scale implies 400,000,000 eligible views at the same rate",
      "reason": "No plausibility cross-check is offered against any reference point (e.g., the real TJR campaign's scale, or the SEA video-commerce market stat already used elsewhere). The deck is careful to avoid top-down TAM math in the market-context section but doesn't apply the same rigor here.",
      "fix": "Add a one-line caveat that 400M views/month is an unvalidated extrapolation of the unit rate, not a market-sized or demand-validated estimate."
    },
    {
      "severity": "low",
      "claim": "Platform revenue recognized as 1,500 MYR (15% of approved rewards) in the 11,500/1,500 breakdown",
      "reason": "Revenue recognition here implicitly assumes payment routing/accounting treatment that the deck elsewhere flags as TBD; without a cross-reference, this section reads as settled.",
      "fix": "Add a footnote at the point revenue is recognized, cross-referencing the 'payment routing/accounting confirmation TBD' disclosure."
    }
  ]
}
```
